ump to keep  everything in an optimal working  condition  it's critical that you choose the right  materials when selecting a freeze dryer  many solvents used in samples can attack  plastic and rubber components which  cause vacuums  to leak over time the use of acids can  also damage materials like stainless  steel and acrylic  once you determine the types of solvents  that will be used with your freeze dryer  you can apply the following criteria to  help you select the right unit  freeze dryers typically come in  collector temperatures of minus 50 minus  84 and minus 105 degrees celsius  keep in mind that the collector  temperature you select should be 15 to  20 degrees colder than the freezing  point of your solvent  in order to effectively trap those  vapors on the collector coil  you also need to determine the collector  size of the freeze dryer  freeze dryers are described by their ice  holding capacity  so if you're looking at a four and a  half liter freeze dryer that means it  can hold four and a half liters of ice  before needing to be defrosted  however this doesn't mean that you can  put four and a half liters of sample on  the freeze dryer at one time  instead you would put about half of that  volume on there to accommodate all of  the vapors that come off of your samples  when  the run starts the next thing to  determine is the type of vacuum pump  that you need  in addition to having the correct vacuum  pump specifications  the solvents being used will determine  whether you operate with an oil rotary  vein pump  a hybrid chemical resistant pump or the  use of a dry scroll pump  there are a variety of ways you can  freeze dry your samples so you also need  to consider the method that's best for  your application in order to select the  correct accessories  besides selecting the correct type of  freeze dryer you also need to select the  right manufacturer  one consideration is how the coil is  designed to collect the vapors coming  from your sample  with our internal coil design we're able  to provide the coldest possible  temperatures  and are superior in capturing the  solvents coming off the samples compared  to an external coil design  that is on the outside of the collector  chamber  a vacuum control valve is another  important feature to have in your freeze  dryer  by being able to set the vacuum level  you're able to speed up the run runtime  when using  certain solvents  other considerations include the types  of controls the freeze dryer has  whether gmp requirements need to be met  and if you need the freeze dryer to have  pre  pre-freezing capabilities  when it comes to controlling your freeze  dryer the software you have is critical  one consideration is how you plan to  access data  with the layer works operating system  you're able to download your data to a  usb flash drive  or use an ethernet connection to send  your data log  straight to your computer through a  one-way connection  remote notification is also available  you have the ability to configure the  types of alerts that can be sent to your  phone via email  this is a great feature to have with  freeze-drying since most runs take 24  hours or longer to complete  you won't be left wondering how your  samples are processing when you leave  the lab for the night  endpoint detection is another feature to  consider if your flask  is pre-shrinking it can be difficult to  tell when these samples are truly done  myopolizing  and it leads people to ending their runs  too soon or running for hours longer  than is necessary  the end zone endpoint detection can help  you avoid both scenarios by alerting you  when your samples have achieved  the level of dryness you set at the  start of the run  now i'm going to pass things over to  jenny so she can discuss developing a  protocol  great thanks megan megan discussed how  to choose a lyapolyzer  now we're going to talk about developing  the protocol there are three steps in  the freeze dry process  pre-