g can occur by controlling your  vacuum pressure you can also speed up  the lyo process  the typical recommendation is 0.2  millibars  for most samples  the ability to run primary drying at  higher product temperatures  greatly reduces drying time by creating  a larger pressure differential  between the vapor pressure over ice in  the product  and the pressure at the condenser each  one degree  increment increase in product  temperature can decrease primary drying  by 13 percent  secondary drying removes the final seven  to eight percent of moisture  that primary drying did not remove it  adds  additional heat to drive the freeze dry  process  secondary drying works best using  controlled heat on product shelf  but can be done in flasks by adding  additional insulation  to warm the flask the temperature can be  as high as 50 degrees c  during secondary drying because the free  ice  is now removed maintaining the same  vacuum pressure  is advised as lower vacuum levels will  not speed up the process  samples can be stoppered under vacuum or  nitrogen for long-term storage  after the freeze dry process is complete  now we'll discuss the best conditions  for the room that the freeze dryer will  be placed in  room temperature should be around 22  degrees c and the room should not be too  small  remember the freeze dryer and the pump  will create heat  on their own keep the unit away from  windows and heat vents  both can cause melting during the  process by warming the sample above the  eutectic point  if the freezer is located far away from  the freeze dryer make sure you insulate  the samples during the walk or drive to  the freeze dryer  to prevent melting before the samples  are placed on the freeze dryer  look for liquid surrounding samples to  determine if meltback occurred  some samples may require additives if  they are not freeze drying correctly  or giving the outcome desired these  include  bulking agents thermal stabilizers lyo  protectants  and ph adjusters