erent between  runs change in room temperature  temperature or purity of the solvents  and the percent of the active ingredient  in the samples can cause these  differences  this is very common in the freeze dry  process  ph shifts are common with buffers  we're ready to take any questions that  you may have  thank you megan and jen for your  informative presentation  we will now start the q a portion of the  webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen we'll answer as many  questions as we have time for  all right let's go ahead and get started  okay first question  is at what temperature must the sample  be  cooled before lyophilization  well it depends on what's  sorry megan go ahead um  that's a really great question but you  know as jenny was starting to say  it just depends on what the sample is  made up of  um so you know we say that  choosing a collector for a freeze dryer  you need to have the sample  temperature differential between 15 and  20 degrees colder than the sample's  freezing point  and you can kind of use the same logic  for  when figuring out what what temperature  you need to freeze your samples at so  if you're doing an aqueous based sample  you know anything below freezing is  typically  fine you know a lot of people have minus  40 freeze dryers that doesn't mean  that you can't use a minus 80 or colder  it just isn't necessary um so don't feel  like you have to go  colder in order to completely freeze  your sample  great thank you all right next question  what is the suggested  room temperature to run lab concho  freeze dryer  yeah so that's a really great question  as well because sometimes  you know we get people that call us and  say they're having trouble with their  freeze dryer you know their samples  melting  all of that kind of stuff and a lot of  times it has to do with the heat load  inside of the room so i will say  that when we 