oose what vacuum setpoint  you want so it really is going to be  dependent  on what your sample is in general we do  recommend the 0.2 millibar  it will help speed up your  lyophilization process  so i think that's what what they were  asking if not i can follow up with an  email just to make sure that we got that  one covered  perfect thank you all right next  question  how can i seal under vacuum without  buying a stoppering tray dryer  we all yeah so oh go ahead jenny  okay what i was going to say is we offer  two different options so if you don't  want to invest in a stop brain tray  dryer or a triad unit  you can either look at a clear manually  stoppered  chamber what that allows you to do is a  clear chamber with two  shelves inside of it you put your vials  on the shelf  and then you manually crank  the stoppering mechanism to raise  those shelves up and stopper or we also  have what's called a mini stop brain  chamber which can be hooked onto  ports of the freeze dryer  i believe you can run up to eight vials  at a time  and you would just stop for under vacuum  but this would be great if you just had  a port type freeze dryer but needed to  stop for  just a few  now what are the suggested containers  glass beakers plastic bags like zip  locks  etc to freeze dry samples  sorry michelle can you repeat that one  more time  sure let me pull it back up  okay what are the suggested containers  to freeze  dry samples for example glass beakers  plastic  bags tubes etc  okay yeah so it really just depends on  what your end goal is  you know if you're trying to prepare  something for long-term storage  or you're working with things like  pharmaceutical research a lot of people  want to stop or under vacuum and so  they'll use  either glass vials or ampule pods that  they can flame feel under a vacuum  if this is just meant as a sample prep  step for you in a series of testing  then you could use something like flask  freeze drying or  trays but anything that's just 