th our internal coil design we're able  to provide the coldest possible  temperatures  and are superior in capturing the  solvents coming off the samples compared  to an external coil design  that is on the outside of the collector  chamber  a vacuum control valve is another  important feature to have in your freeze  dryer  by being able to set the vacuum level  you're able to speed up the run runtime  when using  certain solvents  other considerations include the types  of controls the freeze dryer has  whether gmp requirements need to be met  and if you need the freeze dryer to have  pre  pre-freezing capabilities  when it comes to controlling your freeze  dryer the software you have is critical  one consideration is how you plan to  access data  with the layer works operating system  you're able to download your data to a  usb flash drive  or use an ethernet connection to send  your data log  straight to your computer through a  one-way connection  remote notification is also available  you have the ability to configure the  types of alerts that can be sent to your  phone via email  this is a great feature to have with  freeze-drying since most runs take 24  hours or longer to complete  you won't be left wondering how your  samples are processing when you leave  the lab for the night  endpoint detection is another feature to  consider if your flask  is pre-shrinking it can be difficult to  tell when these samples are truly done  myopolizing  and it leads people to ending their runs  too soon or running for hours longer  than is necessary  the end zone endpoint detection can help  you avoid both scenarios by alerting you  when your samples have achieved  the level of dryness you set at the  start of the run  now i'm going to pass things over to  jenny so she can discuss developing a  protocol  great thanks megan megan discussed how  to choose a lyapolyzer  now we're going to talk about developing  the protocol there are three steps in  the freeze dry process  pre-freezing is your most important step  if the samples aren't frozen  solid melt back will occur knowing your  sample's freezing point is extremely  important primary drying will remove 92  to 93  of the moisture from the sample and will  be the longest step of the process  again you want to make sure your samples  don't melt during this time  finally secondary drying will remove the  residual moisture from your samples  and takes about one third to one half  the time as primary drying  there are different ways to pre-freeze  stub freezing  limits the surface area and is not the  preferred method to pre-freeze samples  in  flasks subfreezing can be put stress  on a flask especially during dynamic  temperature  changes this method is best for serum  vials on a tray dryer  okay slam freezing is a preferred method  of freeze drying  over stub freezing and flasks slam  freezing provides a large  surface area and decreases the freeze  dry run time  shell freezing is the optimal method for  freeze drying in flasks as it maximizes  the surface area to volume ratio  by spreading the sample throughout the  entire vessel  this gives you optimal surface area  and it optimizes the heat input to the  sample  providing a large contact area between  the sample  and the flask  by controlling the rate of pre-freezing  you can change the size of your ice  crystal  pre-freezing results in larger crystals  which freeze dry  faster it's best to know your samples  and how they respond to the freeze dry  process now we'll move on to primary  drying  this step will be the longest and remove  the majority of the moisture from the  sample  during this step adding heat is  favorable to the process  on shelf you have to control how much  heat is added  the shelf temperature should be set to  just below the sample's freezing point  to prevent melting  on ports the heat will come in the form  of temperature from your room  if your freeze dryer is sitting near a  window or other heat source  melting can occur by controlling your  vacuum pressure you can also speed up  the lyo process  the typical recommendation is 0.2  millibars  for most samples  the ability to run primary drying at  higher product temperatures  greatly reduces drying time by creating  a larger pressure differential  between the vapor pressure over ice in  the product  and the pressure at the condenser each  one degree  increment increase in product  temperature can decrease primary drying  by 13 percent  secondary drying removes the final seven  to eight percent of moisture  that primary drying did not remove it  adds  additional heat to drive the freeze dry  process  secondary drying works best using  controlled heat on product shelf  but can be done in flasks by adding  additional insulation  to warm the flask the temperature can be  as high as 50 degrees c  during secondary drying because the free  ice  is now removed maintaining the same  vacuum pressure  is advised as lower vacuum levels will  not speed up the process  samples can be stoppered under vacuum or  nitrogen for long-term storage  after the freeze dry process is complete  now we'll discuss the best conditions  for the room that the freeze dryer will  be placed in  room temperature should be around 22  degrees c and the room should not be too  small  remember the freeze dryer and the pump  will create heat  on their own keep the unit away from  windows and heat vents  both can cause melting during the  process by warming the sample above the  eutectic point  if the freezer is located far away from  the freeze dryer make sure you insulate  the samples during the walk or drive to  the freeze dryer  to prevent melting before the samples  are placed on the freeze dryer  look for liquid surrounding samples to  determine if meltback occurred  some samples may require additives if  they are not freeze drying correctly  or giving the outcome desired these  include  bulking agents thermal stabilizers lyo  protectants  and ph adjusters  if your lab requires gmp for your freeze  dryer  there are a couple things to keep in  mind gmp regulations neither approve nor  prohibit specific equipment for use in  manufacturing  firms are afforded the flexibility to  select equipment that best satisfies  their particular needs and they are free  to modify standard equipment designs to  best  suit their process manufacturers can  provide  the materials of construction a qa test  report  for the specific freeze dryer that was  ordered  manuals videos literature dimensions  etc to help you run your unit  an iqoq is typically offered by the  manufacturer to be done by the customer  however pq must be done by an outside  consulting firm  not all samples are a good fit for a  lyophilizer  these next few slides will talk about  the samples and how to overcome their  challenges  some samples don't give a nice powder at  the end of the run  tert-butyl is one of the results in a  gummy end product  alcohols are difficult to freeze dry  because of the low eutectic temperature  and lack of temperature differential  between the sample and the collector  temperature  they must be diluted to 10 percent  or less and use a minus 105  freeze dryer for successful freeze  drying  dmso is known to attack rubber and  plastic components which are a big part  of any freeze dryer  because of this these parts may need to  be replaced over time to prevent any  vacuum  leaks sugars and salts lower the  eutectic temperature which require a  cascade type  collector they can also bypass the  collector and get into the pump as  particles  an inline hepa filter will protect the  pump from these particles  soil samples are typically aqueous based  however  the particles can migrate into the  vacuum pump and again get into the oil  and cause damage to the pump  an inline hepa filter is also  recommended when doing soil  samples if your samples are fruit and  vegetables  the best way is to homogenize them or if  at the very least  poke holes in the skin to allow a way  for moisture to leave the sample  this is true if you're running animal  samples as well  freeze drying is an art and things may  go wrong during the process  most of the issues can be overcome by  taking a few steps  if your samples are not staying frozen  make sure you're using the correct  collector for your sample  remember a temperature differential of  15 to 20 degrees  is required small samples tend to melt  faster than larger samples  insulating them will help if there are  alcohols in the samples  again make sure you dilute them and make  sure you're using a minus 105 degree  freeze dryer vacuum pressure should not  be too high  if you can't get a good vacuum it may be  time for an oil change  as the samples dry they become powdery  and could get pulled  out under a deep vacuum using filter  paper or parafilm will help prevent this  and remember colder is not always better  when it comes to freeze drying  if the samples are taking too long to  dry check the shelf temperature  and make sure it's close to the sample's  freezing point and not lower  surface area will help speed up the  process if the sample is too  thick it's going to take a long time for  the moisture in the bottom of the sample  to move through the already dried upper  portion  also make sure you have enough space  between the flask and the rubber top  this is especially important if you're  putting test tubes inside of a flask  if there's a not enough space between  the top of the test tube and the rubber  top  it will prevent vapor flow  poor vacuum can sometimes be fixed with  an oil change if one has not been done  for a while  check your oil after each run to make  sure it's not too cloudy or dirty  you can also check the valves and  gaskets for cracks or tears that might  cause a leak  if samples are not sealing correctly  make sure they are evenly spaced over  the shelf  prior to stoppering those samples  many things can cause the final product  to look different between  runs change in room temperature  temperature or purity of the solvents  and the percent of the active ingredient  in the samples can cause these  differences  this is very common in the freeze dry  process  ph shifts are common with buffers  we're ready to take any questions that  you may have  thank you megan and jen for your  informative presentation  we will now start the q a portion of the  webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen we'll answer as many  questions as we have time for  all right let's go ahead and get started  okay first question  is at what temperature must the sample  be  cooled before lyophilization  well it depends on what's  sorry megan go ahead um  that's a really great question but you  know as jenny was starting to say  it just depends on what the sample is  made up of  um so you know we say that  choosing a collector for a freeze dryer  you need to have the sample  temperature differential between 15 and  20 degrees colder than the sample's  freezing point  and you can kind of use the same logic  for  when figuring out what what temperature  you need to freeze your samples at so  if you're doing an aqueous based sample  you know anything below freezing is  typically  fine you know a lot of people have minus  40 freeze dryers that doesn't mean  that you can't use a minus 80 or colder  it just isn't necessary um so don't feel  like you have to go  colder in order to completely freeze  your sample  great thank you all right next question  what is the suggested  room temperature to run lab concho  freeze dryer  yeah so that's a really great question  as well because sometimes  you know we get people that call us and  say they're having trouble with their  freeze dryer you know their samples  melting  all of that kind of stuff and a lot of  times it has to do with the heat load  inside of the room so i will say  that when we test our pre-strayers um at  labconco before we send them out to  customers  we do all of our quality control testing  in a temperature-controlled room at 72  degrees  um and so really anywhere between 68 and  72 is an ideal temperature  to run your freeze dryer  thank you and what is the name of the  accessory that indicates when flask  freeze drying is complete  that would be our end zone endpoint  detection system  and if you have any additional questions  about that feel free to reach out to us  but  the you know like we kind of covered in  the presentation it's really just  um the use of vacuum sensors measuring  the vacuum level inside of the sample  compared to a reference  port on the manifold  thank you and does it matter how i  pre-freeze my samples  pre-freezing is actually the most  important step of the process  you want to get as much surface area as  you possibly can so  shell freezing in flasks is going to be  the highest recommendation if you cannot  shell freeze then slant freezing is  going to be the the next next  thing  great thank you now would you recommend  lyophilizing  products in the 96 well plates and if so  how would you be able to control the  exposure once the door is opened  well so 96v plates can be tricky to  freeze dry because there's a couple  things  first of all the samples are typically  very very small  so if they do melt during the run they  could potentially pop  out also if they're very powdery once  you do vent  the vacuum you could lose uh some of the  powdered sample the end sample  so if you can somehow cover the the  micro plates with either parafilm or a  filter paper  and then poke holes in it that's going  to really help you with  with the whole process and prevent loss  of sample  great thank you now this question says  that you said  0.2 glycerol vacuum is required  does this mean that the minimum 0.2  vacuum required to start freeze drying  process  i hope that makes sense  well with our freeze dryers you can  choose what vacuum setpoint  you want so it really is going to be  dependent  on what your sample is in general we do  recommend the 0.2 millibar  it will help speed up your  lyophilization process  so i think that's what what they were  asking if not i can follow up with an  email just to make sure that we got that  one covered  perfect thank you all right next  question  how can i seal under vacuum without  buying a stoppering tray dryer  we all yeah so oh go ahead jenny  okay what i was going to say is we offer  two different options so if you don't  want to invest in a stop brain tray  dryer or a triad unit  you can either look at a clear manually  stoppered  chamber what that allows you to do is a  clear chamber with two  shelves inside of it you put your vials  on the shelf  and then you manually crank  the stoppering mechanism to raise  those shelves up and stopper or we also  have what's called a mini stop brain  chamber which can be hooked onto  ports of the freeze dryer  i believe you can run up to eight vials  at a time  and you would just stop for under vacuum  but this would be great if you just had  a port type freeze dryer but needed to  stop for  just a few  now what are the suggested containers  glass beakers plastic bags like zip  locks  etc to freeze dry samples  sorry michelle can you repeat that one  more time  sure let me pull it back up  okay what are the suggested containers  to freeze  dry samples for example glass beakers  plastic  bags tubes etc  okay yeah so it really just depends on  what your end goal is  you know if you're trying to prepare  something for long-term storage  or you're working with things like  pharmaceutical research a lot of people  want to stop or under vacuum and so  they'll use  either glass vials or ampule pods that  they can flame feel under a vacuum  if this is just meant as a sample prep  step for you in a series of testing  then you could use something like flask  freeze drying or  trays but anything that's just 